# Fumola, a Work in Progress #

# ^fumola_wip runs a Fumola computation beside Hazel code: a Hazel value #
# goes in, the computation's result comes out, and the result is what #
# the livelit expands to. #

# It is a prototype of a DSL inside Hazel, edited as tiles, inside a #
# livelit. Its name says it is unfinished and slow: editing tiles inside #
# a livelit costs a full view and update per keystroke today. #

# Like ^js (the slide before this one) it ships built in, written in the #
# editor's own implementation language, so it can draw the Fumola tiles #
# and this instance's watch pane itself. #

# The head row names the INSTANCE (myInstance), and picks which of #
# Fumola's two roles the code runs in: editor | archivist. #

# As the ARCHIVIST, the code runs inside a force, in a thunk the #
# archivist's name names (myLivelit here), so what it reads and writes #
# is recorded. Every cell it owns is named under that name: #
#   `myLivelit(`input)    written from Hazel: the In wire #
#   `myLivelit(`compute)  the thunk the code runs in, reused across edits #
#   `myLivelit(`output)   the code's result, read back: the Out wire #
# (`in and `thunk would read better, but both are Fumola keywords.) #
# Left empty, the name defaults to hazel_ and the id of this use. #

# The archivist's input is the CELL, not what it holds: the code reads #
# it with @ input, when and where it chooses. Below, the computation #
# makes two sub-thunks. halve reads the input cell again, inside itself, #
# and halves it. double is given halve unforced, forces it, and doubles #
# the result. So the input is read in halve, not in the computation, #
# and the Outline shows double forcing halve, and halve reading the #
# input: 40, halved to 20, doubled to 40. #

# As the EDITOR, the code runs at the top level of the instance, with no #
# force around it, so it needs no name and owns no cells. Its input is #
# optional: ticked, input is bound to the Hazel value; unticked, as in #
# the second livelit below, input is not bound at all. #

# The second livelit is the editor of the same instance. It asks the #
# instance for its history and returns the events, which cross back as #
# Hazel data: a list of (event = ..., metaTime = ...) records, the same #
# events the Events tab shows. It runs after the archivist, so the #
# archivist's run is in them. #

# Below each head, the full width of the notebook: on the left the In #
# wire, the code, and the Out wire; on the right the watch pane, whose #
# tabs show the Program sent, the Outline of the runs, what they #
# Printed, and the instance's Events, Nodes and Edges. #

# The input is a splice, and it can name a variable bound outside the #
# livelit: n below. It crosses into Fumola as a hazel ... end escape, #
# evaluated when the program runs, where n is in scope. #

# The tiles box switches the code between Fumola tiles and a string. #
# Code that cannot be converted, tiles with a hole in them or text that #
# does not parse, stays as it is. #

# Try: change 20 to 30, or * 2 in double to * 3, untick tiles, or #
# click editor on the first livelit. #
let n = 20 in
let result = ^^livelit(^fumola_wip((instance = "myInstance", name = "myLivelit", editor = false, bind_input = true, tiles = true, input = (n * 2), code = (fumola $graphical as myInstance in
  let halve = `halve` := thunk {
    (@ input) / 2
  };
  let double = `double` := thunk {
    (force halve) * 2
  };
  force double
end)))) in
let events = ^^livelit(^fumola_wip((instance = "myInstance", name = "", editor = true, bind_input = false, tiles = true, input = (0), code = (fumola $graphical as myInstance in
  (prim "adaptonPeekHistory" ()).events
end)))) in
(result, events)
